# Compose a cropper

`Cropper` and `FixedCropper` own image loading, geometry, cleanup and context. Compose their children to choose the crop region; use snippets to replace a visual layer.

<Example {source} title="Child stencil and reactive coordinates">
	<Demo />
</Example>

## Children choose the stencil

With no `children`, a cropper creates a `RectangleStencil`. Supplying children replaces that default entirely. Children without a registered stencil are valid: the image can still be transformed and exported. Put extra guides inside the stencil's own `children` snippet so they share its positioned crop region.

Only one stencil may be registered in each cropper. Conditional replacement preserves the existing crop as far as the new constraints allow. A nested cropper supplies its own context.

<ComponentsHierarchy />

## Rendering layers

The default tree is `CropperWrapper` → `StretchableBoundary` → `CropperBackgroundWrapper` → background image and stencil children. The boundary also contains the internal export canvas. The `wrapper`, `boundary`, `backgroundWrapper` and `background` snippets replace their respective layers.

See [layer composition](/docs/composition/layers), [custom stencils](/docs/composition/stencil-context) and [preview composition](/docs/composition/previews).

## Settings and algorithms

Pass built-in settings directly: `minWidth`, `aspectRatio`, `defaultCoordinates` and `imageRestriction` are cropper props. The `settings` object is reserved for your extension fields; those fields cannot redefine built-in settings. `CropperInstance<E>` preserves the extension type in `getSettings()` and callbacks.

Algorithm props, `postProcess` and `stencilConstraints` let a composed cropper change geometry policy. See [Cropper](/docs/reference/cropper) and [core settings](/docs/core/settings). There is no public headless Svelte controller; an independently managed core integration owns its own loading, reactivity and cleanup.
